  {"op": "index", "title": "P4d-0 ford bar probe", "intro": "Produced by tools/render_jobs/jobs/p4d_probe.json at 1920x1080. A new 150 ft temperate forest map (seed 1234) with a straight waist river at x = -7 and a straight ankle stream at x = 8, saved as _p4d_probe and played; then probes/p4d.gd lays a gravel bar across each at z = 2 (derived geometry: a strip whose crest stands under the water surface by 0.2 m on the river and 0.15 m on the stream, shoulders easing to the bed, the palette's gravel surface, collision on the terrain layer) and tokens are dropped on both bars and in the river off the bar. Captures: home with the grid; zoom 8 on the waist bar plain, with the grid and with the water hidden; zoom 8 on the ankle bar; the waist bar rebuilt 0.35 m deep. Questions: (a) how the water shader reads a submerged bar (edge foam over the crest or only at its edges, refraction and caustics showing the bar as bed, any sorting fault at its edges); (b) whether a token dropped on the bar stands on it ankle deep with no submerged ring, and what the drag ruler and grid read there; (c) what the bar's shadow under the water looks like. The landing numbers are in log.txt (p4d.gd landing)."},
